WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.383
Eradication focuses on removing the cause of the incident, not just quieting the visible symptom.

2
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:14.193
If containment is about limiting harm now, eradication is about preventing the same issue from continuing or returning.

3
00:00:14.343 --> 00:00:20.300
The root cause might be a vulnerable system, an unsafe configuration, an exposed credential,

4
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:25.982
unauthorized access, a malicious artifact, a weak process, or a gap in ownership.

5
00:00:26.132 --> 00:00:29.111
The right fix depends on the analysis.

6
00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:32.665
Sometimes eradication means patching and validating a system.

7
00:00:32.815 --> 00:00:39.623
Sometimes it means rebuilding a host, removing unauthorized access, correcting cloud permissions, disabling an unsafe setting,

8
00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:44.029
or changing a process that allowed the problem to happen.

9
00:00:44.179 --> 00:00:49.711
This work should be authorized and tracked because eradication often touches production systems.

10
00:00:49.861 --> 00:00:58.371
Verification matters: the team should confirm that the cause was addressed and that the environment no longer shows the same

11
00:00:58.521 --> 00:01:00.721
behavior.

12
00:01:00.871 --> 00:01:03.071
Incomplete eradication creates recurrence risk.

13
00:01:03.221 --> 00:01:08.328
A clean-looking dashboard is not enough if the underlying path remains open.

14
00:01:08.478 --> 00:01:16.137
Good eradication produces evidence of the fix, the validation performed, and any residual risk that still needs ownership.

15
00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:21.819
Eradication also depends on knowing when a visible symptom is only a symptom.

16
00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:27.501
Resetting one account may not be enough if the access path remains open.

17
00:01:27.651 --> 00:01:32.757
Removing one artifact may not be enough if the vulnerable configuration remains.

18
00:01:32.907 --> 00:01:39.716
The response team should look for the condition that allowed the incident to happen or continue.
